---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfEditOverlayEditor from '@/components/tools/pdf/PdfEditOverlayEditor.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="annotate-pdf">
  <PdfEditOverlayEditor client:load mode="annotate" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>Marking up a document without a review server</h2>
      <p>
        Commenting on a PDF usually needs a review platform with accounts for everyone and a shared
        workspace, or it is unreliable: a comment made in one reader may not appear in another and
        often does not print. Both problems have one cause. PDF comments are annotations, objects that
        sit on top of the page and not in it, and each reader treats them differently.
      </p>
      <p>
        This tool does not use annotations. Your highlighter strokes, pen marks, boxes and notes are
        drawn into the page content. There is no comment pane to enable, no author identity attached,
        and nothing that can be toggled off or that fails to print. The marked-up PDF behaves like a
        marked-up sheet of paper.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>A workflow for reviewing a draft</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Read first, mark second. Go through once and highlight the passages you will comment on,
          then go back and write.
        </li>
        <li>
          Use colour as a code: yellow for "look at this", red for "this is wrong", green for "agreed,
          no action". Explain the code in a note on the first page, because the file does not record
          it.
        </li>
        <li>
          Put the note in the margin, not over the text. Click in the white space and type. If the
          connection is not clear, drag a line from the note to the passage.
        </li>
        <li>
          Box figures and tables; do not highlight them. A highlight makes a chart darker, and an
          outline box next to it prints clearly.
        </li>
        <li>
          Number your points. Write "1", "2", "3" in the margin and put the detail in a covering email
          to keep a dense page legible.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Drawn marks versus PDF annotations</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[620px]">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col">Drawn into the page (this tool)</th>
              <th scope="col">PDF annotation objects</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Shows in every reader</td>
              <td>Always</td>
              <td>Usually, but appearance varies</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Prints</td>
              <td>Always</td>
              <td>Depends on the reader's print settings</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Survives conversion to image or upload to a portal</td>
              <td>Yes</td>
              <td>Often lost</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Recipient can delete individual marks</td>
              <td>No</td>
              <td>Yes, one click each</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Threaded replies, author names, timestamps</td>
              <td>No</td>
              <td>Yes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Requires an account or a server</td>
              <td>No</td>
              <td>Only for shared review</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        If you received a PDF with annotations from another reader and want to make them permanent,
        <a href="/flatten-pdf">Flatten PDF</a> draws each one into the page from its stored appearance.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Drawing well with a stylus</h2>
      <p>
        The freehand pen uses pointer events, so an Apple Pencil, an S Pen, a Wacom tablet and a Surface
        Pen all work. For a steady result, zoom in before you draw and set the stroke a little thicker
        than you think: 2 to 3 points looks like a firm pen mark, and 1 point looks like a scratch when
        the page is scaled down for print.
      </p>
      <p>
        Strokes are simplified on release: points closer than 0.8 points to each other are removed.
        This keeps a page of dense annotation from making the file large, with no visible change to the
        line.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Privacy when the document is confidential</h2>
      <p>
        Contracts in negotiation, embargoed manuscripts, patient records and internal audits often need
        annotation and should not go to a web server. Nothing here is transmitted: the file is parsed,
        marked up and written in your tab. The tool adds no author name, device identifier or timestamp
        to the file unless you type one.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
